codex
1. **BLOCKER — A signed app is not an authorized user.** The XPC signing requirement authenticates the client binary, but any local account able to run it could ask the root daemon to scan or clean systemwide data. Bind each request to its connection and audit user, enforce per-user access, and require an explicit admin authorization for privileged cleanup. Never accept a scan ID or node ID created by another connection.

2. **BLOCKER — The deletion check has a race.** `lstat` followed by `realpath` followed by deletion lets another process replace a path between checks. Walk from a trusted directory using directory file descriptors and `openat` with no-follow checks; verify each component and delete with `unlinkat`. Refuse mount-point crossings and unexpected ownership. Treat wildcard children under `/Library/Caches` and `/Library/Updates` as untrusted.

3. **BLOCKER — “Known-safe” overstates the cleanup list.** `/Library/Updates`, `/private/var/db/diagnostics`, Google Drive `canceled_uploads`, and agent session directories can contain active, pending, or recoverable data. Remove broad deletion for these targets until each has a documented lifecycle check and a tested recovery path. `log erase --all` is a systemwide log purge, not deletion of one selected directory.

4. **SHOULD — The daemon’s scan API needs resource limits.** A signed client can request repeated root scans and retain large trees indefinitely. Cap concurrent scans, memory, traversal time, response size, and request size; expire scan state on disconnect and validate every `Data` payload before decoding.

5. **SHOULD — The SMAppService plist is underspecified.** Specify and test `Label`, `MachServices` with a Boolean `true`, `BundleProgram`, and the exact `Contents/Library/LaunchDaemons` placement. Sign the helper and outer app in the correct order, then verify the final DMG’s signatures and registration on a clean macOS 14+ machine. Apple’s [SMAppService example](https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/802443) confirms this layout.

6. **SHOULD — Approval and upgrade handling need a state machine.** A daemon requires **admin** approval before launch, and `register()` can report already registered or denied. Unregistering and re-registering on every version mismatch can disrupt approval and active work. Handle each [service status and registration error](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/servicemanagement/smappservice/register%28%29?changes=_4), and test replacement updates with a new helper inode.

7. **BLOCKER — `st_blocks * 512` does not equal reclaimable space on APFS.** Clones share extents; snapshots can retain blocks after deletion; compression and metadata complicate attribution. Label this value “file allocated blocks,” never “what the disk loses,” and measure actual free-space change separately after cleanup. Keep container usage and scanned file totals as different metrics.

8. **SHOULD — The “Unaccounted” calculation mixes accounting domains.** APFS container consumed space includes other volumes, snapshots, metadata, and potentially purgeable space. Subtracting scans from it creates a misleading residual, especially when cloned extents are counted twice. Reconcile by APFS container and volume role, show snapshots and purgeable space separately where measurable, and label any residual as unexplained.

9. **SHOULD — Dataless files may make scanning change the result.** Traversal or metadata calls can materialize cloud-backed directories, causing network access and new disk use. Detect `SF_DATALESS`, avoid reading contents, and disclose that enumeration itself may materialize intermediate folders. Apple documents this behavior in [TN3150](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3150-getting-ready-for-data-less-files?changes=_3).

10. **SHOULD — Snapshot enumeration is not snapshot sizing.** `tmutil listlocalsnapshots` gives a list, not each snapshot’s exclusive bytes or reclaimable size. Do not add snapshot “sizes” to System Data without a supported measurement; show names and a separately sourced aggregate estimate.

11. **SHOULD — The category reconstruction will diverge materially from Storage Settings.** Apple categorizes by app and file type as well as location: iCloud Desktop/Documents, iOS backups, system apps, media purchases, Music Creation, and the designated System Photo Library defeat the proposed globs. Present these as **app-defined categories**, avoid claiming path-level correspondence to Apple’s bars, and add explicit rules for the documented cases. [Apple’s category descriptions](https://support.apple.com/en-au/guide/mac-help/mchl3d437fbc/mac) are the baseline.

12. **SHOULD — User cleanup commands need per-user execution and previews.** Run `simctl`, package-manager commands, Trash operations, and cloud-provider cleanup only in the affected user’s session, never in the root daemon. Discover their actual locations, show the exact items and estimated impact, and treat “prune,” runtime deletion, and empty Trash as irreversible operations.

13. **SHOULD — Verification needs hostile-machine tests.** Add tests with a second local user, forged IDs, oversized XPC payloads, symlink swaps during deletion, bind/mount crossings, hard links, clones, snapshots, dataless folders, and an app update while the daemon is active. These exercise the main failure modes that dummy cleanup targets will miss.
